comic fans have a pretty unhealthy habit  of comparing interpretations of their  favorite characters to one another be it  the age-old which is the best spider-man  argument which cinematic Batman has the  highest kill count or which version of  the Hulk was the most disappointing but  so far as an outsider to the community  I've noticed the TMNT fans either a  don't compare versions of the characters  at all for some reason or be just just  don't really talk about the turtles at  all they're so shy about it it's such a  nice quiet little phantom that just  enjoys seeing the four teenage reptiles  get any representation at all even if  it's the Michael Bay kind over the past  year I watched both of the most recent  completed TMNT shows and I felt like I  had a lot to say about them so imagine  this is a review for both at the same  time hopefully without causing a war and  disturbing the natural order  I can discuss something I see few people  discussing which Ninja Turtles cartoon  was the best however that question comes  with some caveats for a few reasons I  don't want to include the 80s cartoon  into this analysis mainly because it has  a hundred ninety-three episodes on my  Jesus I'm a bandwidth-limited spare time  as it is I can't really devote the time  to all that without this video coming  out in three years additionally that one  feels the most nostalgic for me since I  had quite a few VHS tapes full of  episodes from it growing up while these  other two shows I never really sat down  and like watch them until 2019 they're  both more fresh in my mind and lastly  the 80s animated turtles are really hard  to compare with these other ones because  it made such big strides to be its own  thing with the light-hearted tone the  colorful and well-defined characters and  the kid-friendly action scenes it was a  far cry from what the Ninja Turtles were  intended to be violent murderous  sewer monsters don't [ __ ] cut you to  pieces  there's some crazy weird Frank Miller  books daredevil was there too I guess  but people forget about that I'm also  not including rise of the TMNT because  it's not over yet and I haven't been  able to watch much of it so far I think  it's really cool and I'd be open to  doing a follow up to review it along  with the other two for those that don't  know because they're living in a sewer  the Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles are a  group of four mutated animals that gain  intelligence and humanoid bodies through  an accident involving that green  chemical stuff that you found in a  bottle under the sink and desperately  want to taste they're led by a humanoid  rat named splinter that trained the four  of them in the art of ninjutsu to defend  themselves and stay hidden from a world  that's prejudiced against green people  with shells the details about their  origin beyond that or shaky and heavily  subject to change depending on which  interpretation watch Helens some of the  comics they're the five reincarnated  souls of a ninja master and his sons  that were killed in feudal Japan but  we're not talking about that both the  2003 series and the 2012 series make  strides to blend the lightness of the  eighty show with the darkness of the  comic in varying ratios and I think it's  what makes them both so unique so let's  look at it like the comic book in the  80s cartoon or both of the parents of  the general idea of Ninja Turtles one  contributed the majority of the premise  plot lines and aesthetic while the other  contributed the more defined characters  from their tone and widespread  mainstream appeal both are awesome in  their own right and you have to commend  the cartoon for developing the  personalities of the turtles a little  more as well as giving them their own  color schemes because telling these guys  apart in black and white is a nightmare  if they aren't holding a weapon plus all  the turtles personalities during that  time were the angry one ironically as a  kid I couldn't tell Leonardo and  Donatello apart because I'm colorblind  in that they just look that looks the  same I find it interesting that the 2003  show is almost word-for-word adapting  scenes from the original comic our backs  are up against the wall in some trash  room alley  cornered by the toughest street gang on  the Eastside nobody messes with the  purple dragons especially wearing stupid  Turtle costumes who's wrong we're not  wearing costumes  while the 2012 show tries to emulate and  modernize more stories and characters  from the 80s cartoon  they both do their thing based on the  legacy of their parent of choice if the  2012 show does adapt any stories from  the comics it's very sparingly and with  a different twist with our focus  narrowed to just these two let's compare  and contrast what they both got right  and well what they don't the Ninja  Turtles wouldn't be the famous brand  they are without such iconic characters  everyone has their own favorite because  each one is so different from the rest  Raphael is the edgy angry hothead with a  temper Mikey is the fun-loving party guy  Donatello is the brains the operation  and Leonardo is the the leader the  serious one the responsible I'm gonna  lose a few subs over this but Leonardo  is usually the least interesting one  TMNT 2003 everyone's pretty much where  they should be including Leo the Turtles  banter is usually pretty jokey and fun  making them feel like old friends I'd  like to see you try on it Kim would you  wish for  Linda Junior they're the perfect blend  of serious and comical and I think they  took a lot of cues from the 90s movie to  get that ratio just right I especially  love their look in this show as they  remind me of like an Edie McGinnis  Deadpool comic visually however I think  the 2012 show took the characterization  a little further and added more depth to  each turtle not a lot mind you but more  than they had particularly leo the 2012  series goes out of its way to explain  his desire to be a leader he's more of a  sci-fi and fantasy nerd who enjoys  watching this Star Trek knockoff cartoon  because he admires the main character so  much for being a captain and he also  likes LARPing more  live-action role-playing if being a  giant turtle monster didn't ensure his  virginity was secured that sure did only  losers play dress-up I appreciate them  giving him hobbies him liking comics and  cartoons and nerdy stuff helps him feel  less like a bland over serious chump he  and the other turtles finally have  something to talk about other than just  giving them orders  I've also noticed that the 2012 Turtles  tend to bicker more and not just in a  short sassy way where they just  exchanged a few one-liners I mean really  argue in that regard I feel like their  relationship as brothers is more  believable because they get on each  other's nerves but they also feel more  openly affectionate to each other to  when they're not fighting I feel their  family bond is more fleshed out and real  I also really dig the voice actors they  got for both shows each respective cast  feels perfect for the version they're in  and seeing as how the 2012 show is more  influenced by the 80s cartoon it's  fitting that they got back the original  voice actor for Rafael SIF play  Donatello why do you sound so weird me  sound weird heard yourself lately hey I  do however kind of wish they hadn't  recast Leonardo halfway through the show  after his original voice American Pie  man got canceled for a controversial  tweet so like why does he sound  different you sustained damage to his  throat after that he was some guy who  sounded close enough for three episodes  and then they finally committed to  recasting in his Seth Green I know we  shouldn't trust karai but still I got  the sense she really is fed up with  shredder okay we need to come up with a  plan part one is Mikey stays here you've  been messing up too much lately we can't  take the chance she gave me a clue where  she is t